+### If the score will determine pass or fail
+
+An additional score-use question is: **under a specified future rating plan,
+how often would the scoring rule misclassify a person, and how precise is
+measurement near the cut?** For this illustration, every person receives six
+ratings from two named raters across the three criteria. The true pass cut is
+0 logits; the operational sum rule passes sums of at least 9. The sum uses
+category indices 0–3, so 9 corresponds to an original 1–4 score sum of 15 for
+six complete ratings. These are illustrative decisions, not recommended cuts
+or cuts inferred from the fitted data.
+
+```{r decision-plan}
+decision_plan <- expand.grid(
+  Rater = sort(unique(as.character(toy$Rater)))[1:2],
+  Criterion = sort(unique(as.character(toy$Criterion))),
+  stringsAsFactors = FALSE
+)
+decisions <- classification_table(
+  fit, rating_plan = decision_plan,
+  cut_scores = c(Pass = 0), score_cuts = 9, operational_cuts = 0,
+  theta = c(-1, -0.25, 0, 0.25, 1),
+  theta_weights = c(0.10, 0.20, 0.30, 0.25, 0.15)
+)
+decisions$cuts
+decisions$table[, c("Theta", "Method", "Accuracy", "Consistency", "SEM", "Status", "SourceStatus")]
+decisions$marginal
+```
+
+The five ability points and their masses define an explicitly hypothetical
+discrete population. They are not fitted EAP scores or a continuous-normal
+quadrature rule. Equality enters the higher category: the mass at 0 belongs
+to the true-pass group. Thus the true-fail and true-pass proportions are 0.30
+and 0.70. Without supplied population weights, the function returns conditional
+results but does not invent a population average.
+
+`lee_sum` calculates the finite summed-score distribution for the stated sum
+rule. `rudner_normal` approximates decisions based on an ML ability estimate
+and its stated ability cut. Those operational rules need not agree; the
+expected sum at 0 in `$cuts` is a diagnostic, not an instruction to round a
+cut. Do not rank the two methods' accuracy without first establishing a common
+decision rule. The normal approximation can be poor for short plans or
+extreme abilities. Neither route evaluates the earlier EAP scoring rule.
+
+`SEMAtTrueCut` and the nearby `SEM` values are in ability logits, conditional
+on this calibration and locally independent ratings. They exclude uncertainty
+in estimated facet/step parameters and are not posterior SDs. Consistency
+concerns two conditionally independent repetitions of the same fixed plan;
+it does not generalize to different raters, retake policies or random tasks.
+This API supports ordinary RSM/PCM; two-family GMFRM is outside its scope.
+
+Report an error's denominator as part of its name. For a binary decision,
+`Overclassification` in `$marginal` is the population joint probability of
+false pass, and `Underclassification` is that of false fail. The joint matrix allows
+three different, useful descriptions of each error:
+
+```{r decision-errors}
+error_rates <- do.call(rbind, lapply(
+  split(decisions$classification_matrix, decisions$classification_matrix$Method),
+  function(d) {
+    false_pass <- sum(d$Probability[d$TrueCategory == 1 & d$ObservedCategory == 2])
+    false_fail <- sum(d$Probability[d$TrueCategory == 2 & d$ObservedCategory == 1])
+    joint <- c(false_pass, false_fail)
+    true_group <- c(sum(d$Probability[d$TrueCategory == 1]),
+                    sum(d$Probability[d$TrueCategory == 2]))
+    decision_group <- c(sum(d$Probability[d$ObservedCategory == 2]),
+                        sum(d$Probability[d$ObservedCategory == 1]))
+    # A missing or zero conditioning mass gives an undefined rate, not zero.
+    given_true <- given_decision <- rep(NA_real_, 2)
+    ok <- is.finite(true_group) & true_group > 0
+    given_true[ok] <- joint[ok] / true_group[ok]
+    ok <- is.finite(decision_group) & decision_group > 0
+    given_decision[ok] <- joint[ok] / decision_group[ok]
+    data.frame(
+      Method = d$Method[1], Error = c("False pass", "False fail"),
+      JointProbability = joint, TrueGroupProbability = true_group,
+      RateGivenTrueGroup = given_true, DecisionGroupProbability = decision_group,
+      RateGivenDecisionGroup = given_decision,
+      Status = d$Status[1], SourceStatus = decisions$source$status
+    )
+  }
+))
+rownames(error_rates) <- NULL
+error_rates
+```
+
+For false pass, the denominators are everyone, people truly below the cut,
+and people declared to pass, respectively. For false fail, the last two are
+people truly at/above the cut and people declared to fail. For example, a
+joint false-pass probability of 0.06 in a population with 0.30 true failures
+would mean a conditional false-pass rate of 0.06/0.30 = 0.20; those are
+illustrative arithmetic values, not the fitted results above. Missing method
+probabilities remain missing in the calculation; no `na.rm = TRUE` discards
+them. Zero denominators give `NA`. Retain `Status` and `SourceStatus` as well
+as the rates: numerical availability does not validate the source calibration,
+population, plan or operational cut. Inspect `decisions$notes` and
+`?classification_table` before interpreting or exporting the results.
 
